CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --glucose --glucose-parallel --glucose-threads 2
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^Solving with Glucose Syrup (portfolio, in process)$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
The portfolio backend must agree with the sequential one. A portfolio solver is
nondeterministic in which thread answers and in the model returned, but
satisfiability is not, so asserting the verdict is sound even though the search
is not reproducible.

The solver_text line is required as well, so the test cannot pass by silently
falling back to the sequential backend -- which is exactly what would happen if
the option stopped being plumbed through to solver_factory.

Two threads rather than the default, so the test does not take four cores on a
machine running the rest of the suite.

The portfolio carries neither the POR decision tier (setPORVar is a no-op
there) nor setPolarity, and it refuses assumptions rather than ignoring them,
so agreement with --glucose is a real property to check and not a formality.
